[?25l> The build succeeded. Here's a summary of what was created:

Files created:
- Cargo.toml — depends on aes = "0.8" (pure Rust AES for the DRBG)
- src/params.rs — all SPHINCS+ constants for blake-256s (SPX_N=32, SPX_D=8, etc.)
- src/address.rs — address manipulation + ull_to_bytes, u32_to_bytes, bytes_to_ull
- src/blake256.rs — BLAKE-256 hash (compress, init, update, final, mgf1)
- src/blake512.rs — BLAKE-512 hash (compress, init, update, final, mgf1)
- src/hash.rs — prf_addr, gen_message_random, hash_message (blake variant)
- src/thash.rs — thash with blake256 for 1-block, blake512 for multi-block (simple variant)
- src/rng.rs — AES-256-CTR-DRBG (randombytes_init, randombytes, AES256_CTR_DRBG_Update)
- src/wots.rs — WOTS+ chain generation, chain_lengths, wots_pk_from_sig, wots_gen_leafx1
- src/fors.rs — FORS sign/verify, compute_root, wots_treehashx1, fors_treehashx1
- src/sign.rs — crypto_sign_keypair, crypto_sign, crypto_sign_open, merkle_sign/gen_root
- src/main.rs — KAT transcript generation (BLAKE-512 transcript, 7 iterations)

Key design decisions:
- Used aes crate instead of OpenSSL for AES-256-ECB (pure Rust, no system dependency)
- Addresses stored as [u8; 32] instead of [u32; 8] since the C code accesses them as byte arrays
- unsafe used only for the global DRBG state (matching C's global DRBG_ctx)
- All BLAKE round permutations faithfully reproduced via macros matching the C macro expansion
- The treehashx1_generic function uses a closure + raw pointer to handle the mutable borrow pattern from the C code✓ Created checkpoint 1



